sys_ppu_thread_create: hand slots (thread ids) out round-robin, not lowest-free.

The pin frees the slot of a thread created without SYS_PPU_THREAD_CREATE_JOINABLE when it ends (#409), and the lowest
free slot, hence the same id, went to the next thread at once. Resistance 3 creates its save-data "Check Thread",
"Auto Load Thread" and "SysUtilThread()" in turn, all with flags 0 and all under id 33, and polls such an id
(sys_ppu_thread_get_priority) to learn that its worker has ended: it met the next thread under the id and the first
level load never finished (fps 0, main thread in a sleep loop). lv2 numbers threads upwards. The search for a free slot
now starts after the one handed out last, so an id comes back only after the whole table has been used.

test_ppu_thread_slots creates threads one after another and checks that ids keep rising (fails with the lowest-free
search: every id repeats).

Upstream: not proposed yet (a candidate).

---

diff --git a/runtime/syscalls/sys_ppu_thread.c b/runtime/syscalls/sys_ppu_thread.c
index cfa2488..7e9a086 100644
--- a/runtime/syscalls/sys_ppu_thread.c
+++ b/runtime/syscalls/sys_ppu_thread.c
@@ -81,8 +81,15 @@ static void table_unlock(void)
 
-/* Find a free slot. Returns index or -1. Must be called under lock. */
+/* Find a free slot. Returns index or -1. Must be called under lock.
+ * The search starts after the slot handed out last, so the id of a thread that has ended (id = slot + 1) is not given
+ * to the next thread at once: lv2 numbers threads upwards, and a title that polls a worker's id to learn that it has
+ * ended must not meet a new thread under it. */
 static int find_free_slot(void)
 {
-    for (int i = 0; i < PPU_THREAD_MAX; i++) {
-        if (g_ppu_threads[i].state == PPU_THREAD_STATE_FREE)
+    static int s_next_slot;
+    for (int n = 0; n < PPU_THREAD_MAX; n++) {
+        const int i = (s_next_slot + n) % PPU_THREAD_MAX;
+        if (g_ppu_threads[i].state == PPU_THREAD_STATE_FREE) {
+            s_next_slot = i + 1;
             return i;
+        }
     }
diff --git a/tests/test_ppu_thread_slots.c b/tests/test_ppu_thread_slots.c
index 9a1d074..7175d1f 100644
--- a/tests/test_ppu_thread_slots.c
+++ b/tests/test_ppu_thread_slots.c
@@ -94,2 +94,17 @@ int main(void)
 
+    /* 1b. The id of a thread that ended is not handed to the next one: a title polls an id to learn that its worker
+     *     is done. Ids go upwards; at most one wrap shows up in these creates. */
+    {
+        uint64_t prev = 0;
+        int reused = 0;
+        for (int i = 0; i < PPU_THREAD_MAX / 2; i++) {
+            uint64_t tid = 0;
+            EXPECT_EQ(create(0, &tid), 0);
+            if (prev && tid <= prev) reused++;
+            prev = tid;
+            settle();
+        }
+        EXPECT_EQ(reused <= 1, 1);                               /* at most the one wrap */
+    }
+
     /* 2. All at once: 63 live non-joinable threads fit, the 64th create is refused until some end. */
